      "summary": "RAG: Introduction and Architecture 1. What is RAG? RAG stands for Retrieval Augmented Generation. It combines information retrieval with a Large Language Model ",
      "body": "# RAG: Introduction and Architecture\n\n## 1. What is RAG?\n\nRAG stands for Retrieval-Augmented Generation. It combines information retrieval with a Large Language Model (LLM) to generate more accurate and relevant answers.\n\nInstead of relying only on what the model learned during training, RAG retrieves information from external sources such as PDFs, documents, and databases and provides it to the model as context.\n\n## 1.1 The Problem RAG Solves\n\nLLMs do not always have access to private, company-specific, or recently updated information. They can also generate incorrect answers when they lack relevant context.\n\nRAG helps solve this by searching external documents and providing relevant information to the LLM before generating an answer.\n\n## 1.2 RAG vs Fine-Tuning\n\n\n![image](data:image/png;base64,)\n\n\n\nI would use RAG when an application needs to answer questions using external documents. Fine-tuning is more suitable when I want to change how a model behaves or performs a specific task.\n\n## 1.3 The RAG Architecture: Two Pipelines\n\nRAG has two main pipelines.\n\n### 1. Ingestion Pipeline\n\nThis pipeline prepares documents so they can be searched later.\n\n1. Load documents such as PDFs.\n2. Extract and clean the text.\n3. Split the text into smaller chunks.\n4. Convert each chunk into an embedding.\n5. Store the embeddings and their corresponding text in a vector database.\n\n### 2. Retrieval and Generation Pipeline\n\nThis pipeline finds relevant information and generates an answer.\n\n1. Receive a user's question.\n2. Convert the question into an embedding.\n3. Search the vector database for similar chunks.\n4. Retrieve the most relevant chunks.\n5. Send the retrieved context and question to the LLM.\n6. Generate an answer using the provided context.\n\n### Simple Architecture\n\n    INGESTION PIPELINE\n\n    Documents\n        |\n    Extract Text\n        |\n    Clean Text\n        |\n    Split into Chunks\n        |\n    Generate Embeddings\n        |\n    Store in Vector Database\n\n\n    RETRIEVAL & GENERATION PIPELINE\n\n    User Question\n        |\n    Generate Query Embedding\n        |\n    Search Vector Database\n        |\n    Retrieve Relevant Chunks\n        |\n    Send Context + Question to LLM\n        |\n    Generate Answer\n\n## Summary\n\nRAG allows an LLM to answer questions using external information instead of relying only on its training data.\n\nIt has two main pipelines: ingestion, which prepares and stores document embeddings, and retrieval, which finds relevant information and uses it to generate answers.\n\nThe main idea is simple: **retrieve relevant information first, then generate an answer using that information.**",
